body:
I'm leaning toward to making non-handlers (i.e. Netty core) never call `discard(Some)ReadBytes` or any operations that changes the `readerIndex` of an inbound `ByteBuf`.

Instead, a handler needs to be responsible for preventing the unbounded buffer growth.  I think it will be OK considering it is very likely that the unbounded buffer growth will trigger an OOME during test phase.

However, the proposed idea does not resolve the issue related with `discard(Some)ReadBytes()` making decoder's index variable go out-of-sync.  I have some candidate ideas for that:

### Modify abstract handlers and adapters

Some abstract handlers and adapters like the ones from the codec framework could call `discard(Some)ReadBytes()` for the sake of convenience. For example:

```
class ByteToMessageDecoder {
    void inboundBufferUpdated(...) {
        ByteBuf in = ctx.inboundByteBuffer();
        ...
        discardInboundReadBytes(in);
        ...
    }

    protected int discardInboundReadBytes(ByteBuf in) {
        return in.discardSomeReadBytes(); // Returns the number of discarded bytes
    }
}

class MyDecoder extends ByteToMessageDecoder {
    private int myIndex;

    @Override
    protected int discardInboundReadBytes(ByteBuf in) {
        // Alternatively, you can just do nothing and return 0 to disable auto-discard.
        int discardedBytes = in.discardSomeReadBytes();
        myIndex -= discardedBytes;
        return discardedBytes;
    }
}
```

`ChannelInboundByteHandlerAdapter` could be modified in a similar way.

### Add `ChannelInboundHandler.inboundBufferTrimmed()`

We could add a new event handler method to `ChannelInboundHandler` such as `inboundBufferTrimmed(ctx, int deltaOffset)`. Unlike other event handler methods, it does not propagate its event to other handlers (i.e. no `ctx.fire*()`).

`DefaultChannelHandlerContext` could wrap the `ByteBuf` returned by `newInboundBuffer` to intercept the `discard(Some)ReadBytes()` and invoke `inboundBufferTrimmed()`.

### Add `ChannelInboundHandler.trimInboundBuffer()`

This idea is at somewhere between the first and the second idea.  It's basically about promoting `discardInboundReadBytes()` of the first idea into the first class handler method.  Pipeline calls `trimInboundBuffer()` between every `inboundBufferUpdated()`.  The default implementation provided by `ChannelInboundHandlerAdapter` calls `discardSomeReadBytes()` on the inbound buffer.

### Just let `ChannelInboundHandler.newInboundBuffer()` return a wrapped buffer.

A user can just wrap a buffer and intercept `discard(Some)ReadBytes()` and Netty can provide some useful wrappers.  This also allows us to get rid of `freeInboundBuffer()` because a user can override `free()`.
